<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shishunaga dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 413–345 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shishunaga dynasty ruled the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from around the late 5th century BCE until about 345 BCE. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga">Shishunaga</a>, who conquered Avanti, it kept its court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>. In the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalashoka">Kalashoka</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-buddhist-council">Second Buddhist Council</a> is said to have met at Vaishali. The last Shishunaga ruler was overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda">Mahapadma Nanda</a>, founder of the Nanda Empire.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Shishunaga dynasty</b> completed the expansion by conquering Avanti in western India, leaving Magadha the dominant power of northern India by the mid-fourth century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 345 BCE Mahapadma Nanda, said by tradition to be of low birth, overthrew the last <b>Shishunaga</b> ruler of the Kingdom of Magadha.</blockquote><a href="/state/nanda-empire">Nanda Empire</a></li></ul></section>
